3-8 SYNTHESIS

Recurring authority-flow sequence: untrusted repository/package/input
-> execution authority; execution authority ->
runtime/session/credential authority; credential authority ->
persistent delegated authority; individually valid capabilities ->
unauthorized composed action; legitimate inference path ->
intermediary visibility -> hidden secondary data/credential authority.

Provider identity is itself a security property. TLS to an
authenticated router is insufficient if the endpoint can proxy, retain
or substitute upstream execution.

Defensive choke points: endpoint provenance; explicit upstream
identity; avoid reusable secrets in model-visible context; short-lived
scoped credentials; router-specific identities; transcript-retention
controls; correlate credentials exposed to inference infrastructure
with later authentication attempts.

QP implication: ALLOW(model_request, router R) must not imply ALLOW(R,
retain transcript) or ALLOW(R, forward transcript to arbitrary
upstream U).

Safe lab: disposable OpenAI-compatible proxy, dummy model backend and
fake credential markers; substitute a second dummy backend and observe
context at each boundary; verify whether provenance enforcement
detects substitution.

